{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubaik.github.io/prompts-are-code-how-to-measure-their-value/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I measure prompt quality without a golden dataset?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You do not need a golden dataset to start. You need a consistent outcome label. The simplest version is a human review queue: every hundredth trace gets read by you or a teammate, and you record whether the outcome was good. This gives you a noisy but unbiased signal. A golden dataset is useful later, when you want to run automated evals in CI, but it is not the first step. The first step is deciding what \"good\" means and writing it down."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the difference between a prompt version and a graph version?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A prompt version is the text of a single prompt. A graph version is the structure of the agent — which nodes exist, how they are connected, and what each node does. They are separate because they change for different reasons. You change a prompt to fix a wording problem. You change a graph to fix a decomposition problem. Logging both lets you tell which kind of change caused an outcome shift, which is the whole point."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why not just use an LLM to grade every output?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You can, and many teams do. The problem is that the grader is itself a model with its own failure modes, and it can drift as the model behind it is updated. If you use an LLM grader, pin the grader version the same way you pin the prompt version, and periodically spot-check its labels against human review. Treat it as a noisy sensor, not as ground truth."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "When should I stop measuring and just ship?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "When the cost of measurement exceeds the cost of being wrong. For a pre-product founder, that is almost immediately. For a product with paying users and a support burden, measurement pays for itself quickly. The signal to start is when you find yourself unable to answer \"did that change help?\" from memory. That is the point where a log line is cheaper than a guess."
      }
    }
  ]
}
